      "source": [
        "# **📗 Word Embedding Models**\n",
        "## **📌 Definition**\n",
        "\n",
        "Word embeddings are dense vector representations of words in a continuous vector space where semantically similar words are close to each other.(often synonyms, but not always exactly synonyms.)\n",
        "\n",
        "king – queen  \n",
        "happy – joyful  \n",
        "fast – quick  \n",
        "doctor – nurse\n",
        "\n",
        "will have similar embeddings (numerically close vectors).\n",
        "Unlike one-hot encoding, embeddings capture semantic meaning, context, and relationships between words."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ijTbLn1f63BF"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 🧠 Why do we study Word Embeddings?\n",
        "\n",
        "Because machines don’t understand words — they understand numbers.\n",
        "\n",
        "Before embeddings, we used **one-hot encoding**, where each word was represented as:\n",
        "\n",
        "king → [1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, ...]\n",
        "\n",
        "queen → [0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, ...]\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "But that representation treats all words as **independent**, with **no relationship** between them.  \n",
        "The model can’t know that *king* and *queen* are related in meaning.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## ✨ Word Embeddings solve that problem\n",
        "\n",
        "They convert words into **vectors of real numbers**, such that:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Similar words have **similar vectors**\n",
        "- Relationships are **preserved mathematically**\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "king - man + woman ≈ queen\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "This simple equation works only because the embeddings capture the **semantic relationship** between these words.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## ⚙️ Practical Applications\n",
        "\n",
        "Here’s where embeddings shine in the real world 🌍👇  \n",
        "\n",
        "### 🗣️ 1. Chatbots & Virtual Assistants\n",
        "When a user types something, embeddings help the bot **understand meaning**, not just keywords.  \n",
        "Example:  \n",
        "> “I’m happy” ≈ “I feel great”  \n",
        "The chatbot can reply correctly because embeddings treat them as similar.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🔍 2. Search Engines\n",
        "When you search for “cheap flights”, embeddings help find results for:  \n",
        "> “low-cost airlines” or “budget travel”  \n",
        "Because the words have **similar meanings** in vector space.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### ❤️ 3. Sentiment Analysis\n",
        "Embeddings help models understand **emotional tone**:  \n",
        "> “good”, “awesome”, “fantastic” → similar vectors → all positive  \n",
        "> “bad”, “terrible”, “awful” → similar vectors → all negative\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 📰 4. Text Classification\n",
        "Used in **spam detection**, **fake news classification**, or **product review analysis**.  \n",
        "Embeddings provide **semantic context** for better accuracy.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 💬 5. Translation & Speech Systems\n",
        "**Google Translate**, **Siri**, and **Alexa** all use word embeddings to map meanings across languages.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🧬 6. Deep Learning Models (NLP base)\n",
        "Modern models like **BERT**, **GPT**, and **Transformers** all start with **embeddings**.  \n",
        "Without them, the model can’t process text at all.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "iqsi-IcC1cZU"
      },
      "source": [
        "# **📗 Word Embedding Models Notes**\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1. Why Use Word Embeddings?\n",
        "- Represent words numerically for ML/DL models.\n",
        "- Capture semantic similarity (e.g., “king” ≈ “queen” – gender relation).\n",
        "- Reduce dimensionality compared to one-hot vectors.\n",
        "- Enable transfer learning: pretrained embeddings can be reused across tasks.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# **2. Types of Word Embedding Models**\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## A. Count-based Embeddings : Co occu matrix, lsa\n",
        "\n",
        "## B. Prediction-based Embeddings: word2vec, fastext, glob vec\n",
        "\n",
        "## C. Contextual Embeddings (Deep Learning-based)\n",
        "\n",
        "**------------------------------------------------------------------------------**\n",
        "### A. Count-based Embeddings\n",
        "- Based on co-occurrence statistics of words in a corpus.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Examples:**\n",
        "1. **Co-occurrence Matrix**\n",
        "   - Row = target word, Column = context word\n",
        "   - Value = count of co-occurrence in a window\n",
        "2. **Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA)**\n",
        "   - Uses SVD (Singular Value Decomposition) on co-occurrence matrix to reduce dimensionality\n",
        "\n",
        "**Pros:** Simple, interpretable  \n",
        "**Cons:** High-dimensional, sparse, less effective on rare words\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ugLgZqapU5Va"
      },
      "source": [
        "# **📘 Count-based Word Embeddings**\n",
        "\n",
        "## 📌 Concept\n",
        "\n",
        "Count-based models build embeddings by **counting how often words appear together** in a text.  \n",
        "They rely on the idea that **words appearing in similar contexts have similar meanings**.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 🔹 1. Co-occurrence Matrix\n",
        "A **co-occurrence matrix** shows how often words occur together within a given context (sentence or window).\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Row:** Target word (the word being represented)\n",
        "- **Column:** Context word (the word appearing nearby)\n",
        "- **Value:** Number of times the context word appears near the target word\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "### 🧠 Example: Co-occurrence Matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "**Words:** I, like, machine, learning\n",
        "\n",
        "**Co-occurrence counts** (rows = focus word, columns = context word):\n",
        "\n",
        "|         | I | like | machine | learning |\n",
        "|---------|---|------|--------|----------|\n",
        "| **I**       | 0 | 1    | 0      | 0        |\n",
        "| **like**    | 1 | 0    | 1      | 0        |\n",
        "| **machine** | 0 | 1    | 0      | 1        |\n",
        "| **learning**| 0 | 0    | 1      | 0        |\n",
        "\n",
        "Step by step:\n",
        "\n",
        "Focus word = I\n",
        "\n",
        "Words near “I” (within window 1) → “like”\n",
        "\n",
        "So row for “I” = [0, 1, 0, 0]\n",
        "\n",
        "Focus word = like\n",
        "\n",
        "Words near “like” → “I” and “machine”\n",
        "\n",
        "So row for “like” = [1, 0, 1, 0]\n",
        "\n",
        "Focus word = machine\n",
        "\n",
        "Words near “machine” → “like” and “learning”\n",
        "\n",
        "So row for “machine” = [0, 1, 0, 1]\n",
        "\n",
        "Focus word = learning\n",
        "\n",
        "Words near “learning” → “machine”\n",
        "\n",
        "So row for “learning” = [0, 0, 1, 0]\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Pmlp79zpVP3i"
      },
      "source": [
        "# **2. Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA)**\n",
        "\n",
        "LSA (Latent Semantic Analysis) is used to reduce the dimensionality of the co-occurrence matrix.\n",
        "It captures hidden relationships between words by compressing the information using SVD (Singular Value Decomposition)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
