        "| Aspect(co occurances)       | Description                                              |\n",
        "| -------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n",
        "| **Pros** | Simple, interpretable, easy to compute                   |\n",
        "| **Cons** | High-dimensional, sparse, doesn’t handle rare words well |\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "YsbMgSmbVqf-"
      },
      "source": [
        "💡 Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "Co-occurrence Matrix: Counts how often words appear together.\n",
        "\n",
        "LSA (SVD): Reduces matrix size to capture deeper relationships between words.\n",
        "\n",
        "These methods are static and based on word frequency, not context."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "-C-jqZTf--78"
      },
      "source": [
        "# 📗 Prediction-Based Word Embedding Models\n",
        "### Main Models\n",
        "1. **Word2Vec (Mikolov et al., 2013)**  \n",
        "   - Predicts words from context (CBOW) or context from words (Skip-Gram).  \n",
        "   - Captures semantic and syntactic relationships.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **GloVe (Global Vectors, 2014)**  \n",
        "   - Combines count-based and prediction-based methods.  \n",
        "   - Learns embeddings by factorizing a word co-occurrence matrix.  \n",
        "   - Captures global corpus statistics.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **FastText**  \n",
        "   - Developed by Facebook.  \n",
        "   - Extends Word2Vec with **subword information (n-grams)**.  \n",
        "   - Handles rare and misspelled words effectively.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "OXGKFn8cWTEU"
      },
      "source": [
        "### ⚔️ Word2Vec vs FastText\n",
        "\n",
        "| Feature                         | **Word2Vec**                                                                     | **FastText**                                                                                             |\n",
        "| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n",
        "| 🧠 **Concept**                  | Learns one vector for each **whole word**.                                       | Learns vectors for **subwords (n-grams)** and combines them to form the word’s vector.                   |\n",
        "| 🔤 **Example**                  | For the word `\"playing\"`, it learns a single vector.                             | Breaks `\"playing\"` into parts like `\"pla\"`, `\"lay\"`, `\"yin\"`, `\"ing\"` and learns from all.               |\n",
        "| 📉 **Handles Rare / New Words** | ❌ Fails — if a word wasn’t seen in training, it has **no vector** (OOV problem). | ✅ Works well — can build a vector using **subword pieces**, even for unseen or misspelled words.         |\n",
        "| ⚡ **Speed**                    | Slightly faster (simpler training).                                              | A bit slower — needs to handle multiple subwords.                                                        |\n",
        "| 💡 **Accuracy**                 | Works well for common words.                                                     | Performs better for **morphologically rich languages** (like Malayalam, Tamil, etc.) and **rare words**. |\n",
        "| 🧮 **Based On**                 | Predicts words (CBOW / Skip-Gram).                                               | Based on **Word2Vec**, but adds **subword modeling**.                                                    |\n",
        "\n",
        "### ✅ In Short\n",
        "- Use **Word2Vec** if your vocabulary is clean and limited.  \n",
        "- Use **FastText** if you expect **rare words, spelling variations, or multiple word forms** (like “run”, “running”, “runner”).  \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🧩 Word2Vec Architectures\n",
        "\n",
        "**CBOW (Continuous Bag of Words):**  \n",
        "- The model predicts a **target word** based on the **surrounding context words**.  \n",
        "- 👉 Example: For context `[I, ___, AI]`, predict **\"love\"**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Skip-Gram:**  \n",
        "- The model predicts **surrounding context words** given the **target word**.  \n",
        "- 👉 Example: For target **\"love\"**, predict `[I, AI]`\n",
        "\n",
        "🧠 So when we say  \n",
        "> “Word2Vec predicts words (CBOW / Skip-Gram)”  \n",
        "it means the model learns **embeddings** by predicting **relationships between words** —  \n",
        "either **context → word (CBOW)** or **word → context (Skip-Gram)**.\n"
      ]
    },
